  "notes": "Garden street (south) facade from the view across the railed garden and the street corner view: a long pale brick front, its western two thirds cantilevered a little over the ground floor, a two-storey ribbon of glass with a pale metal spandrel, patches and bands of open-bond perforated brick, a recessed glass slot at the west party wall. North facade from the dusk view: a brick stair tower standing clear of the roof, a lower eastern part with punched windows and brick lattices at ground level, and a second cantilevered volume with two ribbon windows to the west, the entrance under the tower. Plot size estimated from storey and door heights (~36 x 22 m); depth, roof and the side streets were not photographed and are guessed; the preserved washhouse under its glass floor is inside and not shown. The brick is a pale grey-beige, not red; the red brick material is kept for the neighbouring houses against the party walls. Pass of 24 Sep 2026: the grey-beige brick is now palebrick (it was stone); the open-bond perforated brick is drawn as mesh panels over the brick one layer in — two patches on the south cantilever beside the glass ribbon (at the two window bands, as in the street-corner view), three bands on the eastern part, two lattices beside the north entrance, and the slatted screen at the entrance itself (dusk view); the roofs are gravel inside the metal coping; the copper-leaved tree moved to the east end of the north front, where the dusk view has it; more shrubs and a poplar in the railed garden across the street; the two streets drawn as concrete strips. Still guessed: plot depth, roof, the side elevations, the neighbours' real extents. Refined 24 Sep 2026 (three rounds against the street-corner view 14 and the dusk view 00): the open-bond perforated brick, drawn as mesh panels, did not show at all (mesh is drawn open onto brick); it is now the brick face with a staggered pattern of single dark voxels (one hole in four), which reads as the photographs' dotted patches and bands - on the south cantilever beside the ribbon, the three bands on the east part and the north lattices; the stair tower rises a metre higher (to 16 m), clear of both roofs as in the dusk view; the copper beech at the north front is bigger (9 m), reaching the second floor as photographed; the neighbours' tile roofs are shallower (the corner view shows low Castilian pitches). Refined 22 Oct 2026 (3 rounds, photos 00-15, the street views 00 and 01, the corner views 02 and 03; the five biggest mismatches were the windows of the north front's east part, the neighbours' height, the stair tower's height, the south cantilever's depth and the garden's planting): the north front's east part has its windows where the dusk view (00) puts them: 2 x 2.3 m at x 4.2-6.2 on the second floor (z 7.7-10) and at x 7.2-9.2 and 10.4-12.2 on the first (z 4.1-6.4), where the first pass had three on one floor at x 6, 9.5, 13 and one over them; the neighbours rise, the east house to 10.2 m (11.7 to its ridge) and the west one to 8.6 m, so the building no longer stands over two sheds (the street-corner view shows the red-brick house beside it nearly as high as its low part); the stair tower stands 2.1 m above the main roof, 14.5 m overall (the dusk view: door 2.1 m is 95 px, roof 12.2 m, tower top 14.4 m; the second pass's 16 m was too high); the south cantilever stands out 1.0 m, with the glass ribbon set deeper behind it; the garden across the street has five more trees, a poplar among them, eight low shrubs and four lavender tussocks. Still guessed: plot depth, the roof, the side elevations, the neighbours' real extents and windows."
